Docker — Simple Explanation

Docker is a platform used to package an application and all its required dependencies into a container.

For example, your Flask CKD application needs:

Python
Flask
NumPy
Pickle
Your ML model (finalized_model_ckd.sav)
Your application code (app.py)

Instead of installing everything manually on every computer, Docker packages the application into a container.

=====

Without Docker
Your Computer
    ↓
Install Python
    ↓
Install Flask
    ↓
Install NumPy
    ↓
Install Dependencies
    ↓
Run app.py

Sometimes you may get errors like:

ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'flask'

====

With Docker
Your Application
      +
Python
      +
Flask
      +
Libraries
      +
ML Model
      ↓
   Docker Image
      ↓
Docker Container
      ↓
Application Runs

====

Simple example using your CKD project
ML_Projects_Flask/
│
├── app.py
├── finalized_model_ckd.sav
├── requirements.txt
├── templates/
└── Dockerfile

The Dockerfile contains instructions such as:

Use Python
↓
Copy application files
↓
Install requirements.txt
↓
Run app.py

===

Easy definition

Docker = A tool that packages an application with everything it needs so it can run consistently on different computers or cloud platforms.

===

For your project:

Flask CKD Application → Docker Container → Cloud Run / other cloud platform → Public URL

Docker makes your CKD Flask application portable and consistent, and 
the Dockerfile and commands needed to run this project show exactly how the container is built and started.

